  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 309 AM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nAnother day, more heat and humidity to contend with along with the \npotential of morning storms.  \n\nA shortwave across southern Nebraska is fueling clusters of storms \nacross central into eastern Nebraska into northern KS. This activity \nis expected to shift east, approaching northwestern Missouri towards \n10-11Z. This activity is building east towards the core of the low \nlevel jet which extends from the panhandle of Texas to northern \nMissouri. Forecast soundings continue to show a dearth of moisture \nbelow 600 mb, so if showers are able to push east, heat bursts would \nonce again be possible.  In addition, showers would have the \npotential to assist in surfacing the 30-40 knot low level jet. The \nshowers are expected to weaken through the morning.  Southerly flow \nwill once again assist in allowing the atmosphere to rapidly warm \nwith ample surface heating. Have continued the excessive heat \nwarning across central Missouri and have issued a heat advisory \nroughly highway 36 and south with afternoon/evening heat indices \nexpected to climb to 105-110 F.  Left far northern Missouri out of \nthe heat advisory as lingering cloud cover/ convective \ndebris/outflow boundaries associated with convection to the north \nmay make it a bit harder to warm to advisory levels.  \n\nLong standing trough across eastern Colorado into western KS is \nexpected to remain in place much of the week. This trough is \nexpected to lead isolated late day convection today, and more \nwidespread activity on Wednesday as yet another shortwave pushes \neast across the Northern Plains.  This could result in the potential \nfor an MCS Wednesday night into Thursday morning, though guidance is \nvery murky on this potential. There are still enough solutions \nsuggesting this as a possibility to prompt SPC to outlook \nnorthern Missouri in a marginal risk for Thursday morning. \n\nTemperature forecast in the extended depends heavily on how \neffective MCS would be on dragging stationary boundary in the \nvicinity of I-80 southward.  Until then, the heat and humidity will \ncontinue with the potential of extending heat headlines in \ntime.",
